    "question": {
      ":question/id": "9f7ad5ee-e16c-51c6-8134-922c44c52860",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n4 \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} - 4 \\cos{(x )}=- y \\sin{(x )} + 5\\,.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} - 4 \\cos{(x )}&=- y \\sin{(x )} + 5 \\\\ 4 \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + y \\sin{(x )}&=4 \\cos{(x )} + 5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(\\frac{\\sin{(x )}}{4})}_{P(x)}y&=\\underbrace{\\cos{(x )} + \\frac{5}{4}}_{Q(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\frac{\\sin{(x )}}{4}$ and $Q(x)=\\cos{(x )} + \\frac{5}{4}.$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
