      ":question/id": "d9a7f8cb-e6b0-522c-b750-3688fc322259",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2 x - 5 y}{x}\\,,\\ x\\ne 0\\,.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=\\frac{2 x - 5 y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=2 - \\frac{5 y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + \\frac{5 y}{x}&=2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(\\frac{5}{x})}_{P(x)}y&=\\underbrace{2}_{Q(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\frac{5}{x}$ and $Q(x)=2.$",
