      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\cos{(2 x )} - y \\sin{(2 x )} + \\sin{(4 x )}&=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\cos{(2 x )} - y \\sin{(2 x )}&=- \\sin{(4 x )} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} - \\frac{y \\sin{(2 x )}}{\\cos{(2 x )}}&=- \\frac{\\sin{(4 x )}}{\\cos{(2 x )}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} - y \\tan{(2 x )}&=- \\frac{2 \\sin{(2 x )} \\cos{(2 x )}}{\\cos{(2 x )}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(- \\tan{(2 x )})}_{P(x)}y&=\\underbrace{- 2 \\sin{(2 x )}}_{Q(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=- \\tan{(2 x )}$ and $Q(x)=- 2 \\sin{(2 x )}.$",
